  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n/2}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=2y$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n/2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{2y})}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2y/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
